+# Agent Note: Explicit sidebar occurrence retirement
+
+Status: proposed
+
+English | [中文](2026-10-11-sidebar-occurrence-retirement.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Closing through an adopted Session cannot remove dormant saved tabs. Clearing a provider's current tabs also leaves inverse layout operations able to resurrect them. Providers must not reach browser persistence internals, adopt Sessions for cleanup, or destroy unrelated history.
+
+## Proposal
+
+The native sidebar owns a versioned synchronous retirement method. It enumerates saved scopes and adopted stores, validates dormant snapshots without rewriting unsupported fields, and reports each occurrence's outcome. An adopted store is authoritative. Close handlers run before mutation; changing the target or stopping the service blocks that attempt. Verified read-back precedes the store commit; failures attempt byte restoration and never report durable removal.
+
+Retirement maps every history cursor to a layout without the exact retired identifier. Normal undo grouping and unrelated history entries remain, while new actions retain their cursor snapshots. This costs one layout per existing cursor after retirement, stays window-local, and does not change the persisted layout format. It does not blacklist a kind: re-registration may create new occurrences. The sole guide rule and empty-column settling remain native rules.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+Public controller and actual native apply-disposal tests cover dormant and live scopes, released adoption, floating tabs, storage rejection, unsupported snapshots, blocked handlers, fresh registration, and undo/redo. Full GUI regression and installed provider unload/reload acceptance remain integration work. Providers must call the public operation and preserve explicit failed outcomes; no plugin adapter or automatic missing-provider cleanup is part of this change.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+Adopting dormant Sessions introduces content and Agent lifetimes during cleanup. Filtering undo by kind prevents legitimate re-registration, and clearing history destroys unrelated operations. Provider-written storage bypasses native validation and commit authority. Mapping exact occurrences at every existing cursor preserves these boundaries.
+
+## Risks
+
+Retirement snapshots increase window-local history memory. A failed rollback cannot certify persisted bytes, so the operation reports unavailable and the provider must retry explicitly. Installed runtime disposal order and adapter retry semantics require integration acceptance before release.
